$ zenctl check probe --help
Read a zk2 resource as a consumer does; do values arrive in a window?

The consumer-shaped acceptance probe (zk2's since #612, FJ8b): "a probe MUST build its keys the way the product builds them", and a zk2 consumer binds a role to providers and reads through the runtime's consumer (spec §3.2) — so this does exactly that, the address as its binding, the template values as R2 bindings, a state resource's current state read from its owner first (S4). The finding is the NO: exit 0 a value that conforms to its declared type arrived; 1 nothing usable arrived while a complete presence read shows a provider holding its token, or no token visible to this reader, or values arrived and none decoded as their type; 2 the presence read timed out, or the probe could not stand up.

Usage: zenctl check probe [OPTIONS] <SYSTEM/SERVICE> <IFACE[@FP]> <RESOURCE>

Arguments:
  <SYSTEM/SERVICE>
          The provider, `<system>/<service>` — bound as a consumer binds a role, either position `*`

  <IFACE[@FP]>
          `<name>.v<major>`, optionally `@<fingerprint>` (or a prefix of one)

  <RESOURCE>
          The stream, state or event resource: its template, or `<kind token>/<template>`

Options:
      --param <NAME=VALUE>
          A template parameter, `NAME=VALUE` with the value unslugged, repeatable (R2). A parameter not given is a wildcard

      --for <SECS>
          How long a value has to arrive, seconds
          
          [default: 10]

      --contracts <PATH>
          Contracts known offline, repeatable: an authoring file (`*.toml`), a directory of them, or a `.history` root.
          
